#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:2
msgid "Sharing and copying links"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:4
msgid ""
"Every page of this guide has an address of its own, and so does every "
"heading on it. That makes the guide easy to point someone at: copy the "
"address from the browser and send it. This page explains what the "
"addresses look like, how to reach a heading directly, and how to check "
"them from a script."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:10
msgid "What an address looks like"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:12
msgid ""
"The guide lives under ``/docs/``. The front page is ``/docs/``, and every"
" other page adds its place in the sidebar to that: the page called "
"*Signing in*, which sits inside *Your first visit*, is at ``/docs/first-"
"visit/signing-in/``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:16
msgid ""
"Addresses end in a slash. An address typed without the slash is sent on "
"to the one with it, so both work, and the one the browser shows "
"afterwards is the one to copy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:20
msgid ""
"Addresses do not change when the guide is edited and rebuilt, unless a "
"page is moved or renamed. A link to a page that no longer exists leads to"
" the site's not-found page, the same as any other address under "
"``/docs/`` with nothing behind it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:25
msgid ""
"Pictures and files the guide links to, such as the picture on :doc"
":`/kitchen-sink/media` and the accounts file on :doc:`accounts`, have "
"addresses under ``/docs/`` as well. They are served by the same guide, so"
" a link to one works for anyone who can open the guide."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:31
msgid "Linking to a heading"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:33
msgid ""
"Hover over any heading to reveal the link beside it, or tab to it with "
"the keyboard. Following that link adds the heading's name to the address,"
" after a ``#``, and scrolls the page to that heading. Copy the address "
"after following it, and whoever opens it lands on the same section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:38
msgid ""
"The panel called *On this page* does the same thing from the side: every "
"entry in it is a link to a heading, and the entries of sub-headings sit "
"under their parent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:42
msgid "Copying from the browser"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:44
msgid ""
"Copy the address from the address bar once the page has scrolled to the "
"heading. Most browsers also offer *Copy link address* when you right-"
"click a link, which is the quickest way to take the address of a heading "
"from the *On this page* panel without scrolling to it first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:49
msgid ""
"On a phone the address bar may hide part of the address. Choose the share"
" button of the browser instead, which sends the whole of it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:53
msgid "Copying from a script"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:55
msgid ""
"A script that needs a page can ask for it like any other address. The "
"simplest way is ``curl``, and because the guide answers with ordinary "
"pages, its output is a page of the site:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:63
msgid "The same thing from Python, checking only that the page is there:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:65
msgid "check_page.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:82
msgid "Long addresses"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:84
msgid ""
"Some addresses are long, and a line of code that holds one runs past the "
"edge of the page. It scrolls sideways inside its own box instead of "
"pushing the page sideways:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:93
msgid "When a heading moves"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:95
msgid ""
"A heading keeps the same name in the address for as long as its words do "
"not change. Reword a heading and its old address still loads the page, "
"but the browser stays at the top of it, because the section it named is "
"called something else now."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:99
msgid ""
"That is the one time a shared link to a heading goes stale. A link to the"
" page itself, without the ``#``, keeps working."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:103
msgid "Checking that a link works"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:105
msgid ""
"The quickest check of a link is to open it in a private window, where "
"nothing you have signed in to follows you. A link to a page of this guide"
" opens the same whether or not you are signed in."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:109
msgid ""
"A link to the staff guide is different. It opens the staff guide for "
"staff, asks everyone else who has not signed in to sign in, and shows the"
" forbidden page to a regular account. See :doc:`/staff-guide` for who can"
" open it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:114
msgid "Sending the link"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:116
msgid ""
"When you send a link to a page of this guide, nothing else needs to go "
"with it. The page brings its own sidebar, so whoever opens it can move on"
" to the pages around it. If you are sending a link to the staff guide "
"instead, say so, so that the person opening it knows to sign in as staff "
"first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:122
msgid "A link that sends more than one place"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/sharing-links.rst:124
msgid ""
"If you want someone to read two pages in order, send the address of the "
"first. Its *Next* link at the foot of the page leads to the second, when "
"the second follows the first in the sidebar. When it does not, send both "
"addresses, each with a word about why."
msgstr ""

